<br />
<MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Main Tasks of the IOTZOO firmware</MudText>
<MudText>- Make the connected hardware usable.</MudText>
<MudText>- Send an MQTT message when the state of the hardware changes. Example: A motion detector senses movement.</MudText>
<MudText>- Receive MQTT messages to control the hardware. Example: Show the current time on a display.</MudText>
<br />
<br />
<MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Main Tasks of the IOTZOO service</MudText>
<MudText>- Provide a web interface for creating rules.</MudText>
<MudText>- Receive all MQTT messages.</MudText>
<MudText>- Process each message: Check whether a rule applies, run it, and send a response message if needed. That response can trigger another rule.</MudText>
<br />
<br />
<MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Main Tasks of the MQTT broker</MudText>
<MudText>The broker sits between senders (publishers) and receivers (subscribers). It receives all messages and forwards each one to the clients that subscribed to its topic.</MudText>
<br />
<MudText>Clients publish messages to the broker. Other clients subscribe to the topics they are interested in. Every MQTT message has a topic.</MudText>
<MudText>A topic is a text string that messages are published to and subscribed to. Topics are hierarchical and separated by slashes, similar to a file path.</MudText>
<br />
<MudText>Example:</MudText>
<MudText>Topic: iotzoo/project1/timer/every_10_seconds</MudText>
<MudText> Message/Payload: { "DateTime": "03.01.2025 15:13:45", "Time": "15:13:45", "TimeShort": "15:13" }</MudText>
<br />
<MudText>The publish/subscribe model decouples publishers from subscribers. They don't need to know about each other. The broker handles the connection between them.</MudText>
